e they  essentially get to vote twice okay  the other delegates who are there also  voted obviously most likely in the  primaries but they only get to cast the  other ballot based on what their state  voted for so it's not a second vote  they're just there representing to cast  that delegate vote the super-delegates  on the other hand they can go to the  polls on primary day cast their ballot  and there goes one vote towards towards  their candidate then they get to the  convention and they get to cast a  independent vote that is equal to every  other delegate in that room how is that  fair how is that democratic how is that  going with the will of the people and I  know super-delegates most of the time  we'll go with the will of the people but  there is no point to it either you want  to go with the will of the people or you  don't you either go with whoever wins  the popular vote of the primaries and  wins the most delegates state-by-state  or you don't that's the problem here  super-delegates should not have a role  within the Democratic Party or a  Republican Party which they don't but  they shouldn't have a role in politics  they shouldn't exist it shouldn't be a  thing that we have to talk about it  shouldn't be something that the  progressives within the Democratic Party  are having to fight to have removed  there are so many battles that should be  taking place right now both within the  party and outside of it and yet we are  having to have some of our best and  brightest people go to the DNC and try  to convince them to actually strip  super-delegates of their power we still  have a party that refuses to add a ban  on fracking to their platform they voted  that down back in 2016 and yet  here we are rather than talking policy  rather than coming up with a party  platform that works for the party that  has good solid messaging that everybody  wants to hear instead of doing that  we're having fights over super-delegates  still that's not productive and again I  am not in any way trying to undermine  the hard work of people who made this  happen it was a fight that needed to  happen and it had to happen  unfortunately it's not a fight that if  the Democratic Party had done what was  right we would have even had to have had  in the first place we should be talking  policy right now we should be meeting  with the party leaders meeting with the  people running for office in this year's  midterms and coming up with a strategy  but instead we have to go back and  rehash these wounds from 2016 because we  as a party the Democrats just kind of  seem incapable of any kind of solid  reflection on themselves and that can  hurt them now there's progress being  made  obviously stripping super-delegates of  some of their power again great positive  step but it is a baby step okay this is  not a be-all end-all for super delegates  they still have power in any power that  they hold is too much power but but at  least for the time being  they have shown the the party elite the  establishment the leadership have shown  that they're willing to give some  concessions so if they're willing to  give those concessions let's keep going  and they are I mean the people in the  Unity Commission are absolutely pushing  forward they're getting things done this  was just the first battle they had to  wage but they are not done but there's  another flip side of this that I think  needs to be talked about I think right  now the Democrats understand that we  really are looking at a blue wave this  coming November in the midterms  but that blue wave cannot happen if you  do not you know work on the  reunification of the Democratic Party  you know the establishment the  progressives you know the still with her  versus the Bernie bros all of that and I  don't use any of those as derogatory  terms you believe what you believe you  support who you support but all of those  people together have to push the  Democrats into office this November and  in order 